{
 "version": 1,
 "description": "Hand-made relevance judgments for the Week 5 semantic-vs-BM25 study. No listing text: only the query, the MLS listing id and a 0/1/2 grade.",
 "grades": {
  "2": "clearly satisfies the query",
  "1": "partly satisfies it (related, weaker, or only implied)",
  "0": "does not satisfy it"
 },
 "method": "20 queries were written before any search was run. For each, a pool of 10 to 19 candidate listings was assembled by the assistant from keyword and classical (TF-IDF/LSA) retrieval runs and then shuffled, so the grader saw listings in an order that did not reveal which system found them. One annotator (the AI assistant) read each candidate's full remarks and graded it against the query text only. 305 judgments in total (204 graded 2, 65 graded 1, 36 graded 0).",
 "limits": [
  "Single annotator, no second opinion: grades are consistent but not independently verified.",
  "Only pooled listings are judged. A listing outside the pool counts as 0 and is reported as unjudged@10 (about 15% of the top 10 of BM25 and of the LSA stand-in); label_judgments.py judges them.",
  "No transformer model was available when the pool was built, so listings that only a transformer would surface (for example MiniLM) are under-represented. Re-run label_judgments.py after changing encoder.",
  "The pool is rich in relevant listings (about two thirds graded 2), so P@k is high for every system; compare systems with each other, not against an absolute target.",
  "20 queries is small: differences of a few points are within noise."
 ],
